	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-mehr">
		<div class="vs-three">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:18px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">One joke, three reactions</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-mehr">Why humour is more than a good joke</h2>
				<p class="dh-rise">The same joke can make one person laugh, irritate another and hurt a third. The text is the same in all three cases. What differs is how it is understood, who tells it to whom and in what situation.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="dh-rise">
				<div class="vs-react">
					<div><i style="--c: var(--lime)"></i><b>laughs</b><span>gets the punchline and experiences it as harmless</span></div>
					<div><i style="--c: #e6dcd5"></i><b>hesitates</b><span>finds no coherent second reading or does not know the reference</span></div>
					<div><i style="--c: var(--peach)"></i><b>is hurt</b><span>experiences the punchline as an attack on themselves or others</span></div>
				</div>
				<ul class="vs-qs">
					<li>How is the joke interpreted?</li>
					<li>How do the people involved relate to one another?</li>
					<li>In what situation is it told?</li>
					<li>Which norms and which culture apply?</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-was">
		<div class="vs-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Subject matter</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-was">What research studies under the name of humour</h2>
			<p class="dh-rise" style="max-width:68ch">“Humour” is a collective term for very different phenomena. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin &amp; Ford, 2018</a>)</span> It can mean someone telling a joke, someone laughing at it, a relaxed attitude to life or the way we deal with difficult things. So anyone talking about humour should first clarify which side they mean.</p>
		</div>
		<p class="vs-sub dh-rise">How people use and experience humour</p>
		<div class="vs-facets">
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Active</span><b>Producing humour</b><p>Telling jokes, quick-witted replies, giving a situation a comic twist.</p></div>
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Receptive</span><b>Understanding and enjoying humour</b><p>Getting a punchline, smiling at something, noticing the comedy in a situation.</p></div>
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Attitude</span><b>Humour as a way of seeing the world</b><p>A cheerful, relaxed view of yourself, of others and of your own mistakes.</p></div>
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Coping</span><b>Humour as coping</b><p>Using humour to gain distance from what weighs on you. Not every form of it does you good. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q10">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span></p></div>
		</div>
		<p class="vs-sub dh-rise">How humour arises and works</p>
		<div class="vs-facets vs-facets--3">
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Construction</span><b>Mechanisms and structures</b><p>Surprise, double meaning, exaggeration, wordplay, timing: humour can arise in very different ways.</p></div>
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Mind</span><b>A neurocognitive process</b><p>Forming expectations, noticing contradictions, rearranging meanings. This can be measured in the brain. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q9">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2015</a>)</span></p></div>
			<div class="vs-facet dh-rise"><span class="k">Feeling</span><b>An emotion</b><p>Amusement is an emotion in its own right. Laughter is its best-known expression, but not its only one. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin &amp; Ford, 2018</a>)</span></p></div>
		</div>
		<p class="vs-sub dh-rise">Typical forms in everyday life</p>
		<div class="vs-phen dh-rise" style="margin-top:8px"><span>Jokes</span><span>Spontaneous comedy</span><span>Irony</span><span>Satire</span><span>Play and teasing</span><span>Laughing together</span><span>Self-irony</span></div>
		<div class="vs-not">
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Laughter is not the same as humour.</b>In observational studies, much of the laughter in conversation follows perfectly ordinary remarks, not punchlines. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q2">Provine, 2000</a>)</span> Laughter is often a social signal.</div>
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Humour is not always audible.</b>You can find something funny without laughing, for example when reading alone or in a situation where laughing would be inappropriate.</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" id="perspektiven" aria-labelledby="h-persp">
		<div class="vs-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Research overview</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-persp">Key perspectives in humour research</h2>
			<p class="dh-rise" style="max-width:68ch">None of these perspectives explains all humour. They shed light on different sides of the same phenomenon and often complement each other. More recent reviews try to bring them together. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q3">Warren et al., 2021</a>)</span></p>
			<p class="vs-legend dh-rise"><span><span class="vs-tag bef">Finding</span>studied empirically</span><span><span class="vs-tag the">Theory</span>interpretation or explanatory model</span><span><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>not yet resolved</span></p>
		</div>
		<div class="vs-lenses">
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">01</span><h3>Incongruity and resolution</h3><p class="frage">Why is surprise so often at the heart of a punchline?</p><p><span class="vs-tag the">Theory</span>Humour can arise when something does not fit our expectations and an alternative interpretation becomes possible. Classically described as a two-stage process: first irritation, then resolution. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q4">Suls, 1972</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>Not every surprise is funny. What else is needed for incongruity to become humour is disputed.</p><span class="vs-plan">In-depth article planned: what is an incongruity?</span></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">02</span><h3>Benign violation</h3><p class="frage">Why do we sometimes laugh at things that are actually wrong?</p><p><span class="vs-tag bef">Finding</span>A violation of expectations, norms or boundaries can seem funny if it is also experienced as harmless or acceptable. In experiments, this depended on psychological distance and personal involvement, among other things. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q5">McGraw &amp; Warren, 2010</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>What counts as “harmless” varies greatly between people and contexts. That makes predictions in individual cases difficult.</p><span class="vs-plan">In-depth article planned: what does benign violation theory explain?</span></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">03</span><h3>Superiority and social comparison</h3><p class="frage">Do we laugh because we feel superior to others?</p><p><span class="vs-tag the">Theory</span>Some experiences of humour involve a feeling of superiority or come at the expense of others. The idea goes back to the philosophy of antiquity. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin &amp; Ford, 2018</a>)</span> Disparaging humour can make prejudice seem more normal within a group. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q6">Ford &amp; Ferguson, 2004</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag the">Limit</span>A lot of humour works without a victim, such as puns or absurd comedy. Superiority explains part of it, but not all forms.</p><span class="vs-plan">In-depth article planned: humour and power</span></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">04</span><h3>Relief and arousal</h3><p class="frage">Does humour have to do with tension and its release?</p><p><span class="vs-tag the">Theory</span>Humour can be associated with a release of tension or a change in emotional arousal. Early models described this as discharge, later ones as a rise and fall in arousal. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">Martin &amp; Ford, 2018</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>The original discharge models are considered outdated. None of this amounts to a blanket promise that humour reduces stress.</p><span class="vs-plan">In-depth article planned: humour, stress and arousal: what is established</span></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">05</span><h3>Play, the social and evolution</h3><p class="frage">Why does humour exist at all?</p><p><span class="vs-tag the">Theory</span>Humour can be studied in connection with play behaviour, social relationships and communication. Evolutionary approaches trace laughter back to play signals in primates. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q7">Gervais &amp; Wilson, 2005</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>Evolutionary explanations are research approaches. They can only be tested indirectly and should not be told as an established origin story.</p><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2025/11/21/warum-wir-lachen-und-warum-humor-ein-evolutionaeres-trainingsprogramm-fuer-den-geist-ist/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): humour and evolution, why do we laugh …</a></article>
			<article class="vs-lens own dh-rise"><span class="n">06</span><h3>Cognition and reinterpretation</h3><p class="frage">What does our mind do when a punchline “clicks”?</p><p><span class="vs-tag bef">Finding</span>Violated expectations, the salience and accessibility of meanings, and reappraisal are central perspectives. Garden-path jokes make these processes particularly easy to study. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q8">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2013</a>; <a href="#q9">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2015</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag off">Open</span>Exactly how understanding and amusement are linked in the brain has only partly been researched.</p><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2026/08/21/homo-probabilisticus-wie-predictive-processing-unsere-wahrnehmung-praegt/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): Homo Probabilisticus, how predictive processing shapes our perception</a></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">07</span><h3>Context and culture</h3><p class="frage">Why is the same joke funny one moment and out of line the next?</p><p><span class="vs-tag bef">Finding</span>Relationship, situation, norms, power relations and cultural expectations influence what is experienced as funny or hurtful. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q3">Warren et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#q6">Ford &amp; Ferguson, 2004</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag the">Limit</span>Context explains a lot, but it is hard to capture fully. That is why every assessment remains an approximation.</p><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2025/07/24/warum-mehr-humor-nicht-automatisch-besser-ist/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): why more humour is not automatically better</a></article>
			<article class="vs-lens dh-rise"><span class="n">08</span><h3>Personality and humour styles</h3><p class="frage">Why do people laugh at such different things?</p><p><span class="vs-tag bef">Finding</span>People differ in how they use humour, for example in affiliative, self-enhancing, aggressive or self-defeating ways. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q10">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span></p><p class="grenze"><span class="vs-tag the">Limit</span>Humour styles describe tendencies. They do not reliably predict how someone will react in a particular situation.</p><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-und-persoenlichkeit/' ); ?>">The four humour styles in everyday life</a></article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-cog">
		<div class="vs-cog">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:18px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Humour as a cognitive change of perspective</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-cog">The mind commits. The punchline forces it to think again.</h2>
				<div class="vs-prose dh-rise">
					<p>When we read or listen, we do not wait until a sentence is over. We commit early to the most likely meaning and build a picture of the situation from it, a mental model.</p>
					<p>Garden-path jokes exploit exactly that. They lead us “up the garden path” to an obvious reading and overturn it at the end. What was <em>salient</em> at first, meaning immediately present, has to give way to a less accessible meaning. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q8">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2013</a>)</span></p>
					<p>Because the beginning, the turning point and the resolution can be clearly identified in this type of joke, it is well suited to experiments. Together with Annekathrin Schacht, Dr Bastian Mayerhofer developed a probabilistic model <span class="ref">(<a href="#q8">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2013</a>)</span> and used EEG to study the time course of comprehension. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q9">Mayerhofer &amp; Schacht, 2015</a>)</span></p>
				</div>
				<div class="vs-papers dh-rise"><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2026/08/21/homo-probabilisticus-wie-predictive-processing-unsere-wahrnehmung-praegt/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): Homo Probabilisticus, how predictive processing shapes our perception</a><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2026/08/21/belief-revision-wenn-richtiges-denken-auf-falschen-annahmen-beruht/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): belief revision, when sound reasoning rests on false assumptions</a><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2025/10/25/dopamin-humor-die-kunst-der-vorhersage/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): dopamine, humour and the art of prediction</a></div>
			</div>
			<div class="vs-joke dh-rise">
				<p class="dh-kicker">An example</p>
				<blockquote>“I want to die peacefully in my sleep, like my grandfather. Not screaming and terrified, like his <em>passengers</em>.”</blockquote>
				<ol>
					<li><b>Expectation:</b> grandad, bed, a peaceful end.</li>
					<li><b>Error:</b> “passengers” does not fit the picture.</li>
					<li><b>Reappraisal:</b> grandad fell asleep at the wheel. “In my sleep” now means: while driving.</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="dh-small" style="color:#c4d7db">A deliberate example of dark humour: whether you laugh at it depends on the person and the context. That is exactly the topic further down.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-model">
		<div class="vs-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">The De:Humore working model</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-model">Prediction, error, revision</h2>
			<p class="dh-rise" style="max-width:68ch">What happens in your head when a punchline lands? This working hypothesis shows you the process in five steps: from expectation through surprise to a new interpretation. It helps you understand humour better, but it is one perspective among several and does not explain every joke.</p>
		</div>
		<figure class="vs-model dh-rise">
			<ol class="vs-flow" aria-label="Working model in five steps">
				<li><b>PREDICTION</b><span class="de">Expectation</span><span>You sense what comes next and build a picture of the situation.</span></li>
				<li><b>ERROR</b><span class="de">Mismatch</span><span>Suddenly something does not fit your picture.</span></li>
				<li><b>REVISION</b><span class="de">Reappraisal</span><span>You spot a second, surprising interpretation.</span></li>
				<li><b>HUMOUR</b><span class="de">Amusement</span><span>This aha moment can make you laugh.</span></li>
				<li><b>ADAPTATION</b><span class="de">Adjustment</span><span>You see things differently from before, and that is where the learning lies.</span></li>
			</ol>
			<figcaption><b>In short:</b> you expect something, it turns out differently, you reinterpret it and perhaps laugh. Afterwards, you see the situation with different eyes. This is a working hypothesis, and not every joke follows exactly this sequence.</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-ctx">
		<div class="vs-ctx dh-rise">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:18px">
				<p class="dh-kicker">Social and context-dependent</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2" id="h-ctx">Humour happens between people.</h2>
				<p>Whether a joke connects or hurts is not decided by the text alone. Nor does intention alone decide its effect. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q6">Ford &amp; Ferguson, 2004</a>)</span> The Humour Risk Check looks at five dimensions for this.</p>
				<div class="dh-actions"><a class="dh-btn" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-risk-check/#check' ); ?>">Start the Humour Risk Check</a><a href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2025/07/24/warum-mehr-humor-nicht-automatisch-besser-ist/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">Why more humour is not automatically better (in German)</a></div>
			</div>
			<ul>
				<li><b>Topic &amp; punchline</b><span>What are we laughing at, and how is the punchline built?</span></li>
				<li><b>Intention &amp; function</b><span>Is the joke meant to connect, provoke, criticise or put others down?</span></li>
				<li><b>Perspective &amp; target group</b><span>Who is making fun of whom, from the inside or the outside?</span></li>
				<li><b>Power relations</b><span>Who has more status, and can the people affected answer back?</span></li>
				<li><b>Context</b><span>Does this humour suit this moment and this audience?</span></li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-lernen">
		<div class="vs-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Back to the key question</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-lernen">What can we learn about the human mind if we understand why it laughs?</h2>
			<p class="dh-rise" style="max-width:68ch">Humour is a window into processes that reach far beyond jokes. The following connections are perspectives for research and application, not guaranteed effects.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="vs-learn">
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Expectations</b><span>How does our brain make predictions, and what happens when they fail?</span></div>
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Mental models</b><span>How quickly can we discard a picture of the situation and build a new one?</span></div>
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Changing perspective</b><span>What links a punchline to reinterpreting difficult situations?</span></div>
			<div class="dh-rise"><b>Social interaction</b><span>How do people attune to one another through shared laughter?</span></div>
		</div>
		<div class="vs-papers dh-rise" style="margin-top:20px"><a class="vs-deep" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2026/05/21/adaptive-systemintelligenz-warum-humor-fussball-und-improvisation-mehr-gemeinsam-haben-als-wir-denken/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">In depth (in German): adaptive system intelligence, humour, football and improvisation</a></div>
		<p class="vs-frog dh-rise">“Analysing humour is like dissecting a frog. <s>Few people are interested and the frog dies of it.</s>”<span class="v">With the right disposition, it is a hell of a lot of fun.</span><small>After E. B. White. The reply is by Dr Bastian Mayerhofer.</small></p>
	</section>

